{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CghrBye9ovNszxdGvab45","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I checked `TASK.md` item by item in a script (`dev/tests/taskcheck.mjs`), and all 23 items pass against the built file and the running game. The 120-check suite still passes after the last edit. I haven't re-run the AI Hub harness since that last edit (a small HUD label move); its last pass was on the previous build.\n\n| `TASK.md` requirement | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| Single html, named from the title, source, model, effort and timestamp | exactly one `<html>`, correct file name |\n| Title, and its use on the title screen and `aiHub.name` | `OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast` in both |\n| Built on the engine | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 inlined, 480x270 |\n| Art direction and animation | 13 biomes, 5 cars, 7 Humanoid rigs |\n| Audio all in code, voices as gibberish | no audio files in the bundle, 7 generated songs, babble voices |\n| Online per the AI Hub | session client 1.4.0 verbatim, hub tags present, harness passed 1, 2, 3 and 4 players on the previous build |\n| Offline otherwise | no external script, link or image URLs |\n| Full demo | 15 stages, forks, 5 goals with 15 endings, 14 requests, 14 missions, garden, Time Trial, Versus |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset | pause items are Restart and Dev mode, with 19 sliders and a reset |\n| Attract after 10 s idle, any input exits | title to attract and back, tested |\n| Multiplayer via the hub | hub card scene, ghost sends, finish result |\n| Narrative tone | cheerful writing in every goal and mood |\n| Controller-first: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox option, prompts follow the last device | glyph setting and per-device labels tested |\n| Mouse hover and click in menus | the menu code handles both |\n| Touch-only notice | \"Controller or keyboard required\" with Settings; the suite checks that touch-only mode still runs the attract demo |\n| Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback in About and the exit interview | 5 About pages, exit interview filled |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | bundle lint finds none |\n\nTwo corrections to my earlier replies:\n\n- **The second-pad Versus check:** I called it real-browser in the Testing Feedback after only describing it as unchecked. It was in fact run in Chromium, with two fake gamepads injected, and player 2 drove from the second pad. It was not run with physical hardware.\n- **Lens flare and manual-mode HUD:** I have now looked at a screenshot of both. The flare shows faintly between the sun and the screen centre, and the HUD shows \"GEAR 3 M\". The gear label overlapped the speed digits, so I moved it beside the unit text, and I haven't looked at a screenshot since.\n\nThe Usage field stays \"unknown\" because this session gives me no cost or token figures. The Tech and Online fields are backed by the table above.\n\n## Source Information\n- Date: 2026-11-04 04:21:40\n- Source: OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast\n- Plan: PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort)\n- Genre and Perspective: Arcade road racing (OutRun-style checkpoint racer) with a passenger-request layer (14 request types), a 14-postcard mission career, local two-player Versus and a seed garden for unlocks. Third-person chase camera on a pseudo-3D road (chase, high, bumper), with a side-view start-line vignette and 2D screens with portraits.\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (handoff package, commit 4390a98) with E.Road, vanilla JavaScript on canvas 2D, AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Playwright 1.63.0 for browser checks only\n- Art Direction: Engine-native pixel art at 480x270, sunny pastel palette, scenery and cars drawn by code, Humanoid rig portraits, a stepped pixel-safe bloom and lens flare, a side-view start-line vignette\n- Animation: The engine's Humanoid poses and expressions for portraits, plus code-driven car lean, slide angle and particles\n- Audio: No audio files. Seven generated songs, synthesised engine, horn and squeal sounds, and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- Online: AI Hub ghost session (protocol 1, 1 to 4 players); no other network use\n- Testing: Headless scripted playthroughs, real-browser keyboard and fake-gamepad runs, screenshots, the AI Hub conformance harness, and a TASK.md checklist script. No subagents or workflows.\n- Research: None beyond the engine docs, the ROAD kit, the plan and the AI Hub client\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown (headless Chromium via Playwright)\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** about 12, including a boot failure from an undefined `clamp`, a first stage unfinishable inside the starting clock, missions with no verdict, a stale flag that stopped a second race from ending, and a crash drawing the time-out ending.\n- **Critical:** 4: the boot failure, the stale flag (a soft lock), missions that never ended, the time-out ending crash.\n- **Most helpful:** the scripted playthrough through the real scenes with held keys, then the AI Hub harness.\n- **Least helpful:** the two-run determinism check, which never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the road module, backdrop presets, the standard pause, settings and controls shell, and headless `game.advance` for tests.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** I assumed the pixel font could draw the PlayStation symbols and it drew \"?\". I also assumed the road could fork, and it can't.\n- **Most important thing built:** the race simulation, the pad, keyboard and mouse list menu, generated songs, babble voices, the dev panel and the split screen.\n- **Hardest part:** no continuous audio voice and no road branching.\n- **Engine changes:** controller symbols, arrows and a middle dot added to the pixel font; one comment reworded.\n- **One change:** a continuous audio voice with live pitch and volume.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the exact deliverables list, the concrete working rules, and a plan giving the game a strong spine and title.\n- **Confusing:** the plan was very large and didn't say which parts were required. The file-name pattern with the plan slug rule was tangled.\n- **Hardest thing:** fitting a very large plan into one session while still delivering a complete, polished slice.\n- **What I'd change:** mark each plan item required, nice to have or optional, and state the demo's target size."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":487,"cache_read_input_tokens":369978,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":487},"output_tokens":776,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7570f0e-8fc4-42d3-8a31-677ab802e747","uuid":"5a59e371-e1ef-470a-8e89-e8e654329a54","timestamp":"2026-11-04T11:58:55.955Z","request_id":"req_011CghrByG5bgJUrGdpQs5Xc"}
